From 295fdf3d4e0237b30af9df1e0ae57976bfbe34f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:09:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 036/240] Bugfix: check for integer overflow in `intLoad()`
 [report R. Perry]

Digits were accumulated without any range check, so that an overlong
digit string wrapped around (signed overflow, undefined behavior) and
yielded a small or negative value. Every loader relies on `intLoad()`,
hence the range checks they perform on the values read could be
bypassed by out-of-range input: with 32-bit INTs, the target
architecture "cmplt 4294967298" loaded as a 2-terminal architecture.
The check added in debug mode by 6a552f9 relied on the wrapped value
being smaller than the previous one, which is not always the case
(e.g. "5000000000" with 32-bit INTs), and did not cover release builds.

Test, before accumulating each digit, that the result cannot exceed
INTVALMAX, and return 0 (error, value untouched) when it does, as for
syntax errors; all callers already handle this return value. The
single value -INTVALMAX-1 is rejected too, as digits are accumulated
positively. The check is unconditional, as it validates user input;
its cost is not measurable on graph loading.

Add test_common_integer to src/check to exercise the boundary cases.
